     "result_text_tail": "ose the board approve rises from $4M to $5M.** The worst combination modelled is 80% of planned volume, ad spend at 32% of sales (the typical figure for brands at this scale) and a 25% import duty. It needs $4.6M at peak, which would have broken the $4M limit. At 38% ad spend the need would be $6.4M and would go back to the board. That is still about a third of the board's $18M cap.\n- **The $40M test is softer.** A and B are now marked \"partly met\" rather than failed, and the deck notes that the board's phrase \"on the way to $40M\" could be read loosely. No option reaches $40M by 2030.\n- **Smaller fixes:**\n  - The scorecard title now says \"of the two US routes\", since Japan also passes the cash and margin tests.\n  - The claim about the US competitor Saebom now matches the pack: it added retail stores in 2025, the year it reached about $30M of US revenue.\n  - The early-warning trigger for ad spend drops to 28% of sales, just under the level needed to get cash back by 2030.\n  - The \"corrected\" retail scenario is labelled as illustrative.\n  - Rounding notes were added to the per-unit figures.\n\nThe decisions I made in your absence are unchanged and listed on the last slide. The cash limit ($5M now) and the trigger levels are my proposals, not board figures. I still couldn't view the slides, since nothing here can display them. Layout was checked in code only, so please page through in PowerPoint before it goes out.\n\nFiles are in `./deliverable/`:\n- `Onsoo_US_entry_board_deck.pptx`"
